[207.627413, "o", "1:## Investigation Brief 04 — Correction Notice Precedence\r\n3:Controller memo (binding reconciliation rule):\r\n5:The corrections desk ships multiple notices per field. When several notices\r\n6:target the same transaction and field, the notice with the lexicographically\r\n7:greatest `effective` date wins. For each winning notice, `previous_value` in\r\n8:`reconciliation_report.jsonl` is the field value immediately before that notice\r\n9:applies (after ledger, amendments, and emails). Owner corrections override\r\n12:## Investigation Brief 04 — Correction Notice Precedence — field notes\r\n14:Site visit #195 to corporate accounting captured mailbox exports showing how intercompany netting dispute correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n22:Interview #629 with corporate accounting highlighted how intercompany netting dispute statements were forwarded without the matching ledger row. Counsel advised preserving the full"]
[207.627548, "o", " thread because downstream exception coding depends on mail timestamps.\r\n24:Draft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n26:Lead reviewer L. Bergstrom noted that intercompany netting dispute created reconciliation noise in corporate accounting during April settlement. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n28:In April settlement, external advisors reviewed intercompany netting dispute and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative context so auditors can explain why a given transaction received compliance_hold.\r\n30:In April settlement, external advisors reviewed intercompany netting dispute and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative context so auditors can explain why a g"]
