[455.464899, "o", "=== Brief 03 directive excerpt (around line None) ===\r\n"]
[455.465066, "o", "(no marker found)\r\n\r\n=== Brief 04 directive excerpt (around line None) ===\r\n(no marker found)\r\n\r\n=== Brief 06 directive excerpt (around line 1060) ===\r\n1058: ## Investigation Brief 06 — Effective Dates on Output\r\n1059: \r\n1060: Auditor directive (mandatory for extract):\r\n1061: \r\n1062: Auditors compared effective_date columns to correction paperwork. Start from the\r\n1063: ledger `date` field (YYYY-MM-DD on output). When a winning correction notice\r\n1064: sets field `date` or `status`, use that notice's `effective` date as\r\n1065: `effective_date` only if that correction modified status or date; otherwise\r\n1066: keep the ledger date. `amount_usd` is numeric from the ledger with two decimal\r\n1067: places in CSV and as a JSON number.\r\n"]
[455.465196, "o", "1068: \r\n1069: ## Investigation Brief 06 — Effective Dates on Output — field notes\r\n1070: \r\n"]
[455.465228, "o", "1071: Follow-up #977 confirmed that external audit had been using an informal spreadsheet for revenue cutoff testing. The formal extractor must instead derive rows only from the canonical ledger and structured sections at file end.\r\n1072: \r\n1073: Memo WP-3912 summarizes stakeholder interviews about revenue cutoff testing. Participants disagreed on whether unsigned amendments should ever override ledger owners; Brief 05 resolves that question for the extractor.\r\n1074: \r\n1075: Follow-up #283 confirmed that external audit had been using an informal spreadsheet for revenue cutoff testing. The formal extractor must instead derive rows only from the canonical ledger and structured sections at file end.\r\n1076: \r\n1077: Memo WP-9083 summarizes stakeholder interviews about revenue cutoff testing. Participants disagreed on whether unsigned amendments should ever override ledger owners; Brief 05 resolves that question for the extractor.\r\n1078: \r\n1079: Interview #648 with external audit highlighted how revenue cutoff test"]
[455.465247, "o", "ing statements were forwarded without the matching ledger row. Counsel advised preserving the full thread because downstream exception coding depends on mail timestamps.\r\n1080: \r\n1081: Follow-up #929 confirmed that external audit had been using an informal spreadsheet for revenue cutoff testing. The formal extractor must instead derive rows only from the canonical ledger and structured sections at file end.\r\n1082: \r\n1083: The committee packet references revenue cutoff testing as a contributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n1084: \r\n1085: During September fieldwork, J. Huang circulated a draft finding on revenue cutoff testing. Finance operations pushed back, arguing the issue was transient cache state; QA reproduced the drift on a cold run the next morning.\r\n1086: \r\n1087: Lead reviewer M. Chen noted that revenue cutoff testing created reconciliation noise in external audit during September fieldwork. Staf"]
[455.465266, "o", "f initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n1088: \r\n1089: Risk assessment WP-2640 ties revenue cutoff testing to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n1090: \r\n1091: Working paper WP-7221 documents a three-way match failure tied to revenue cutoff testing. The team escalated when repeated extractor runs produced different status columns for the same transaction id.\r\n1092: \r\n1093: The committee packet references revenue cutoff testing as a contributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n"]
[455.465287, "o", "1094: \r\n1095: Draft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n"]
[455.465301, "o", "1096: \r\n1097: Risk assessment WP-3166 ties revenue cutoff testing to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n"]
[455.465566, "o", "1098: \r\n"]
[455.465602, "o", "1099: Interview #874 with external audit highlighted how revenue cutoff testing statements were forwarded without the matching ledger row. Counsel advised preserving the full thread because downstream exception coding depends on mail timestamps.\r\n1100: \r\n1101: In September fieldwork, external advisors reviewed revenue cutoff testing and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative context so auditors can explain why a given transaction received compliance_hold.\r\n1102: \r\n1103: Draft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n1104: \r\n1105: Draft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n1106: \r\n1107: The committee"]
[455.465621, "o", " packet references revenue cutoff testing as a contributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n1108: \r\n1109: Follow-up #357 confirmed that external audit had been using an informal spreadsheet for revenue cutoff testing. The formal extractor must instead derive rows only from the canonical ledger and structured sections at file end.\r\n1110: \r\n1111: Memo WP-9051 summarizes stakeholder interviews about revenue cutoff testing. Participants disagreed on whether unsigned amendments should ever override ledger owners; Brief 05 resolves that question for the extractor.\r\n1112: \r\n1113: Working paper WP-4856 documents a three-way match failure tied to revenue cutoff testing. The team escalated when repeated extractor runs produced different status columns for the same transaction id.\r\n1114: \r\n1115: Site visit #886 to external audit captured mailbox exports showing how revenue cutoff testing correspondence arrived betwee"]
[455.465639, "o", "n two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n1116: \r\n1117: Memo WP-6590 summarizes stakeholder interviews about revenue cutoff testing. Participants disagreed on whether unsigned amendments should ever override ledger owners; Brief 05 resolves that question for the extractor.\r\n1118: \r\n1119: During September fieldwork, L. Bergstrom circulated a draft finding on revenue cutoff testing. Finance operations pushed back, arguing the issue was transient cache state; QA reproduced the drift on a cold run the next morning.\r\n1120: \r\n\r\n=== Brief 07 directive excerpt (around line None) ===\r\n(no marker found)\r\n\r\n=== Brief 08 directive excerpt (around line None) ===\r\n(no marker found)\r\n\r\n=== Brief 09 directive excerpt (around line 1672) ===\r\n1670: ## Investigation Brief 09 — Mid-Year Amendment\r\n1671: \r\n1672: Committee ruling (binding for extract):\r\n1673: \r\n1674: Mid-year QA found compliance holds disappearing after date corrections. This\r\n1675: amendment "]
[455.465659, "o", "supersedes any earlier wording about hold timing. For `compliance_hold`\r\n1676: only, compare each qualifying email's `sent:` date against the transaction's\r\n1677: ledger date immediately before correction notices are applied — that is, the date\r\n1678: in effect after ledger load, meeting amendments, and compliance mail merges, but\r\n1679: before any correction notice changes `date` or `status`. Brief 03 still governs\r\n"]
[455.465684, "o", "1680: mail-driven status changes; this amendment governs only the hold flag.\r\n1681: \r\n1682: ## Investigation Brief 09 — Mid-Year Amendment — field notes\r\n"]
[455.465703, "o", "1683: \r\n1684: During July review, A. Ndiaye circulated a draft finding on capital project capitalization. Finance operations pushed back, arguing the issue was transient cache state; QA reproduced the drift on a cold run the next morning.\r\n1685: \r\n"]
[455.465719, "o", "1686: Lead reviewer L. Bergstrom noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n"]
[455.465736, "o", "1687: \r\n"]
